<section class="sec" id="sec-groups">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">07</span><h2>Pile Groups &amp; Efficiency</h2></div>

  <p>Piles are almost never used singly &mdash; they come in groups under a common cap. And a group of nine piles does <em>not</em> carry nine times a single pile.</p>

  <div class="analogy">
    <span class="an-icon">&#129746;</span>
    <div>
      <strong>Drinking straws sharing one milkshake.</strong> One straw draws from all the fluid around it. Put nine straws close together and they compete for the same soil &mdash; their zones of influence overlap, so each is less effective than it was alone. Spread them further apart and interference falls, which is exactly why minimum spacing rules exist.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-vio">
      <span class="vtag">Converse&ndash;Labarre efficiency</span>
      <div class="eq">\[ \eta = 1 - \frac&#123;\theta&#125;&#123;90&#125;\cdot\frac&#123;(n-1)m + (m-1)n&#125;&#123;mn&#125; \]</div>
      <p class="small">&theta; = arctan(D/s) in degrees, m &times; n piles at spacing s. Group capacity Q<sub>g</sub> = &eta; &middot; (mn) &middot; Q<sub>u</sub>.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-rust">
      <span class="vtag">Block failure &mdash; the other check</span>
      <p>In clay a closely spaced group can fail as one enormous block rather than as individual piles. Compute the capacity of the group perimeter acting as a single deep footing, and <strong>take the smaller</strong> of the two answers.</p>
      <div class="eq">\[ Q_&#123;g(block)&#125; = c_u N_c^* (B_g L_g) + \sum \alpha c_u\, p_g \Delta L \]</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="why-matters">
    <span>&#9889;</span>
    <div><strong>Minimum spacing is typically 2.5&ndash;3.5D</strong>, most commonly 3D. Closer and efficiency drops sharply while driving one pile heaves or damages its neighbours; further apart and the pile cap grows expensive. At s = 3D a 3&times;3 group has &eta; &asymp; 0.73 &mdash; you lose over a quarter of the theoretical capacity to interference.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 8 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-neg">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">08</span><h2>Negative Skin Friction</h2></div>

  <p>Skin friction is normally your friend: the soil grips the pile and holds it up. But if the <em>soil</em> settles more than the <em>pile</em>, that grip reverses direction and starts dragging the pile down.</p>

  <div class="grid2">
    <div class="vcard v-rust">
      <span class="vtag">When it happens</span>
      <p>A pile driven through a soft clay layer that is still consolidating &mdash; under a new fill, a lowered water table, or its own weight. The clay settles past the pile, and friction along that length becomes a <strong>downward load</strong> instead of an upward resistance.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb">
      <span class="vtag">The double penalty</span>
      <p>You lose the friction you were counting on over that length, <em>and</em> you gain an extra load the pile must carry. A layer that should have contributed +200 kN might instead impose &minus;200 kN &mdash; a 400 kN swing. Mitigation: bitumen coating on the shaft through the settling layer, or a sleeve.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="core">
    <span>Core Idea</span>
    <p>Negative skin friction is a <strong>load</strong>, not a reduction in resistance. Add it to the applied structural load before comparing against allowable capacity &mdash; do not simply subtract it from Q<sub>u</sub>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 9 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-test">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">09</span><h2>The Pile Load Test</h2></div>

  <p>Every equation in this chapter is an empirical correlation with real scatter &mdash; capacity predictions are commonly off by 30% or more. The load test is the only way to actually find out, and on any significant project it pays for itself.</p>

  <div class="grid3">
    <div class="vcard v-teal"><span class="vtag">Procedure</span><h4>Load in increments</h4><p>Apply load in steps of roughly 25% of the estimated allowable load, holding each until settlement stabilises, and record the load-settlement curve.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-amb"><span class="vtag">Interpretation</span><h4>Finding Q<sub>u</sub></h4><p>Sometimes there is a clear plunging failure. More often there is not, so a criterion is used &mdash; for example net settlement reaching 10% of the pile diameter, or Davisson's offset line.</p></div>
    <div class="vcard v-vio"><span class="vtag">Value</span><h4>Design confidence</h4><p>A verified capacity permits a lower factor of safety (often 2 instead of 3), which frequently saves more in pile lengths across the site than the test cost.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 10 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-examples">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">10</span><h2>Worked Examples</h2></div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 1</span><h4>Driven Pile in Sand</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> A 0.4 m square precast concrete pile is driven 12 m into sand with &gamma; = 18 kN/m&sup3;, &phi;' = 32&deg;. Take K = 1.4, &delta;' = 0.7&phi;', and a critical depth of 15D. Find the allowable capacity with FS = 3.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>Geometry:</strong> \( A_p = 0.4^2 = 0.16\text&#123;m&#125;^2 \), perimeter \( p = 4(0.4) = 1.6\text&#123;m&#125; \), critical depth \( L_c = 15(0.4) = 6.0\text&#123;m&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Effective stress at the tip is capped at L<sub>c</sub>:</strong> \( q' = 18(6.0) = 108\text&#123;kPa&#125; \) &mdash; not 18(12) = 216 kPa</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>End bearing</strong> with Meyerhof's N<sub>q</sub>* = 81 at &phi;' = 32&deg;: \( Q_p = 0.16(108)(81) = 1400\text&#123;kN&#125; \), but the limiting value is \( 0.16(0.5)(100)(81)\tan32^\circ = 405\text&#123;kN&#125; \). <strong>The limit governs: Q<sub>p</sub> = 405 kN.</strong></div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Skin friction, 0 to 6 m</strong> (&sigma;'<sub>v</sub> rising 0 &rarr; 108, average 54): \( Q_&#123;s1&#125; = 1.4(54)\tan(22.4^\circ)(1.6)(6) = 299\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>5</b><div><strong>Skin friction, 6 to 12 m</strong> (&sigma;'<sub>v</sub> constant at 108): \( Q_&#123;s2&#125; = 1.4(108)\tan(22.4^\circ)(1.6)(6) = 598\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>6</b><div>\( Q_u = 405 + 299 + 598 = 1302\text&#123;kN&#125; \), so \( Q_&#123;all&#125; = 1302/3 = 434\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Q<sub>all</sub> &asymp; 434 kN. Shaft friction supplies 69% of capacity. Note the lower 6 m contributes twice as much friction as the upper 6 m &mdash; not because it is deeper, but because &sigma;'<sub>v</sub> has already reached its capped value there.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 2</span><h4>The Same Pile in Clay</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> The same 0.4 m &times; 12 m pile is instead driven into clay with c<sub>u</sub> = 60 kPa. Take &alpha; = 0.55. Find the allowable capacity with FS = 3.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div><strong>End bearing:</strong> \( Q_p = 9c_u A_p = 9(60)(0.16) = 86.4\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div><strong>Skin friction:</strong> \( Q_s = \alpha c_u\, p L = 0.55(60)(1.6)(12) = 633.6\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div>\( Q_u = 86.4 + 633.6 = 720\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div>\( Q_&#123;all&#125; = 720/3 = 240\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Q<sub>all</sub> = 240 kN &mdash; only 55% of the same pile in sand. The tip contributes just <strong>12%</strong>: in clay this is essentially a friction pile, and its capacity is very nearly proportional to its length.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="example">
    <div class="ex-h"><span>Example 3</span><h4>A 3 &times; 3 Pile Group</h4></div>
    <p class="prob"><strong>Problem:</strong> Nine of the clay piles from Example 2 are arranged in a 3 &times; 3 group at 1.2 m centres (s = 3D). Find the group capacity using Converse&ndash;Labarre.</p>
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step"><b>1</b><div>\( \theta = \arctan(D/s) = \arctan(0.4/1.2) = \arctan(0.3333) = 18.43^\circ \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>2</b><div>\( \eta = 1 - \dfrac&#123;18.43&#125;&#123;90&#125;\cdot\dfrac&#123;(3-1)(3)+(3-1)(3)&#125;&#123;3\times3&#125; = 1 - 0.2048\left(\dfrac&#123;12&#125;&#123;9&#125;\right) = 1 - 0.273 = 0.727 \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>3</b><div><strong>Group capacity:</strong> \( Q_g = \eta(mn)Q_u = 0.727(9)(720) = 4711\text&#123;kN&#125; \), so \( Q_&#123;g,all&#125; = 4711/3 = 1570\text&#123;kN&#125; \)</div></div>
      <div class="step"><b>4</b><div><strong>Also check block failure</strong> &mdash; the group perimeter is 2.8 m &times; 2.8 m. In clay this check can govern for closely spaced groups, and the smaller of the two answers must be used.</div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="ans"><strong>Answer:</strong> Q<sub>g</sub> &asymp; 4711 kN ultimate, 1570 kN allowable &mdash; against 9 &times; 720 = 6480 kN if the piles did not interfere. <strong>27% of the theoretical capacity is lost to group interaction</strong>, which is exactly why a pile cap cannot simply be designed pile-by-pile.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- 11 -->
<section class="sec" id="sec-quiz">
  <div class="sec-h"><span class="sec-n">11</span><h2>Quick Reference &amp; Quick Check</h2></div>

  <div class="qref">
    <div><span>Ultimate capacity</span><span>Q<sub>u</sub> = Q<sub>p</sub> + Q<sub>s</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>End bearing, sand</span><span>A<sub>p</sub>q'N<sub>q</sub>* &le; A<sub>p</sub>(0.5p<sub>a</sub>N<sub>q</sub>*tan&phi;')</span></div>
    <div><span>End bearing, clay</span><span>9c<sub>u</sub>A<sub>p</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Skin friction, sand</span><span>f = K&sigma;'<sub>v</sub>tan&delta;'</span></div>
    <div><span>Skin friction, clay</span><span>f = &alpha;c<sub>u</sub></span></div>
    <div><span>Critical depth</span><span>15&ndash;20 D</span></div>
    <div><span>K (driven / bored)</span><span>&asymp;1.4 / 1&minus;sin&phi;'</span></div>
    <div><span>&delta;'</span><span>0.5 to 0.8 &phi;'</span></div>
    <div><span>Group efficiency</span><span>Converse&ndash;Labarre &eta;</span></div>
    <div><span>Minimum spacing</span><span>2.5&ndash;3.5 D (usually 3D)</span></div>
    <div><span>FS</span><span>3 (2 with load test)</span></div>
    <div><span>Negative skin friction</span><span>added as a load, not a strength loss</span></div>
  </div>

  <div class="quiz" id="quiz">
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>1. A pile in sand is driven to 30 m instead of 12 m. Why doesn't capacity increase as much as expected?</p>
      <button data-v="0">The pile buckles under its own weight</button>
      <button data-v="1">Below the critical depth (15–20D), &sigma;'<sub>v</sub> stops increasing, so extra friction accrues linearly rather than quadratically</button>
      <button data-v="2">Sand becomes weaker with depth</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>2. Why does &alpha; decrease as clay becomes stiffer?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Stiff clay has lower c<sub>u</sub></button>
      <button data-v="1">The pile is smoother in stiff clay</button>
      <button data-v="2">Driving remoulds and softens a thin annulus of clay around the shaft, and stiff clay loses proportionally more strength</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="0">
      <p>3. A pile passes through soft clay that is still consolidating under a new fill. What happens to the friction along that length?</p>
      <button data-v="0">It reverses to negative skin friction — a downward load added to the applied load</button>
      <button data-v="1">It doubles, since the clay is compressing onto the pile</button>
      <button data-v="2">It is unaffected; friction always resists</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="1">
      <p>4. A 3×3 group at 3D spacing has &eta; = 0.73. What does that mean?</p>
      <button data-v="0">Only 73% of the piles were installed correctly</button>
      <button data-v="1">The group carries 73% of nine individual piles, because their stress zones overlap</button>
      <button data-v="2">The factor of safety must be raised to 1/0.73</button>
    </div>
    <div class="q" data-a="2">
      <p>5. In Example 2 the tip supplied only 12% of capacity. What does that imply for design?</p>
      <button data-v="0">The pile should be replaced by a shallow footing</button>
      <button data-v="1">End bearing can be ignored in all pile design</button>
      <button data-v="2">It is essentially a friction pile — capacity scales with length, so shortening it is costly</button>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="quiz-score" class="quiz-score"></div>
</section>

